::STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Four students are equally eligible to present first. What probability should each student have in a fair method?

{
=A. 1/4#Correct. Four equally eligible students should each receive one of four equal chances.
~B. 1/2#This gives each student too large a chance because there are four students, not two.
~C. 1/8#This gives each student too small a chance because only four students are eligible.
~D. 4/1#This is greater than 1, so it cannot represent a probability.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
A random number from 1 to 60 is used to choose one of three groups fairly. Which assignment is fair?

{
~A. Group A: 1-30, Group B: 31-45, Group C: 46-60#Group A receives more numbers, so the groups do not have equal chances.
=B. Group A: 1-20, Group B: 21-40, Group C: 41-60#Correct. Each group receives 20 of the 60 possible numbers.
~C. Group A: 1-10, Group B: 11-40, Group C: 41-60#Group B receives more numbers than the other groups.
~D. Group A: 1-15, Group B: 16-35, Group C: 36-60#The ranges have different sizes, so the method is not fair.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Which method is not fair for choosing one student from a class when every student should have the same chance?

{
~A. Drawing one identical name slip from a box#Identical slips give equal chances when each student has one slip.
~B. Using a complete roster and a random number generator#A complete roster with equal number assignments can be fair.
=C. Choosing the first student who raises a hand#Correct. This favors students who respond fastest or are most confident.
~D. Numbering every student once and choosing a random number#This can be fair because each student receives one number.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
Ten equal prize slips are placed in a box and one slip is drawn. What must be true for the draw to be fair?

{
~A. The winner must be the same person each time#A fair random draw does not guarantee the same result each time.
~B. The slips must be different colors#Different colors could create bias if the person drawing can see them.
~C. The box must contain more than ten slips#The number of slips should match the intended chances, not simply be larger.
=D. Each eligible person must have the same number of identical slips#Correct. Equal numbers of identical slips create equal probabilities.
}

::STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.3
Question:
A method gives Student A probability 0.50 and Students B, C, D, and E probability 0.125 each. Why is this unfair if all five students should have equal chances?

{
=A. Student A has a larger probability than the others#Correct. Equal eligibility requires equal probability for each student.
~B. All probabilities must be greater than 0.50#Probabilities do not need to be greater than 0.50; they need to match the intended chance.
~C. Five students cannot be selected randomly#Five students can be selected randomly if the method is designed fairly.
~D. The probabilities add to more than 1#The probabilities add to 1, but the distribution is unequal.
}
